{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 5. Apply a downward level adjustment (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a regime shift downward, the model reverts too slowly and keeps prices elevated. Increase responsiveness to recent negative momentum (e.g., weight last 5–10 days more).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: model projects stronger/longer rebounds than realized (peaks too high, declines too shallow). Damp rebound amplitude and shorten rebound duration.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (roughly monotonic). Use a lead-time scaling haircut (small days 1–3, moderate days 4–10, largest days 11–30).",
    "Underestimates drawdown depth: during selloffs the realized path falls further and stays lower than forecast. Add a downside stress adjustment when recent volatility is high and the last 10–15 days trend is negative."
  ]
}
